fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1070", description: some(value: "AN OPERATION'S OWN DESCRIPTION BLOCK IS PARSED AND SILENTLY DROPPED — the grammar\naccepts it, the IR has nowhere to put it, and NO `DescriptionInfo` fact is emitted.\n\nMEASURED (WI-1000), two spellings against one sentinel string, counting\n`anthill.reflect.DescriptionInfo` facts after a full load:\n\n  describe show {< SENTINEL >}                     1 hit    -- standalone `describe`\n                                                            DescriptionInfo(target: show, …)\n\n  {< SENTINEL >}                                   0 hits   -- the operation's OWN block\n  operation show(x: Rec) -> Int64 = 7\n\nWHERE IT IS LOST, exactly: `grammar.js` `operation_declaration` opens with\n`repeat(field('description', $.description_block))`, and `operation_entry` (the\n`operation { … }` block form) does not even have that. `parse::ir::Operation` has no\n`descriptions` field — compare `AbstractSort.descriptions` and\n`SortWithBody.descriptions`, which both exist and both reach `emit_desc_fact` — so\n`convert_operation` never reads the field and the text is gone before the loader.\nNothing warns. `grep -n '\"description\"' parse/convert.rs` finds four readers; none\nof them is an operation.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS BEYOND TIDINESS. §4.1 makes a description block \"free-form text,\npreserved as KB facts\", and the reflect layer is what a tool reads to document an\nAPI — so today an operation, the single most documentation-worthy declaration in the\nlanguage, is the one that cannot carry documentation attached to itself. It also\nforced a decision elsewhere: 059 R3 admits a standalone `describe` in a secondary\nentry whose target the entry declares, and one of the reasons recorded for admitting\nit rather than refusing it outright is precisely that a `describe` is TODAY THE ONLY\nWAY to document a member (WI-1000; docs/proposals/059-secondary-entries.md, the R3\nclassification table). Closing this gap does not overturn that verdict — the rule is\nkeyed on the TARGET, not on the absence of an alternative — but the reason should be\nre-read at the site when it does.\n\nSCOPE: add `descriptions: Vec<String>` to `parse::ir::Operation`, read the field in\nthe operation converter (both the long form and `operation_entry`, adding the\n`repeat(field('description', …))` the block form lacks), and emit `DescriptionInfo`\nfrom `load_operation` the way `load_abstract_sort` already does for a sort. Check\nwhether a `const` has the same hole while there (`parse::ir::Const` likewise carries\nno `descriptions`) and say so either way.\n\nACCEPTANCE: an operation's inline `{< … >}` emits a `DescriptionInfo` naming that\noperation, DRIVEN by reading the fact back — not by asserting the program loads;\nsame for the `operation { … }` entry form; a control shows the standalone `describe`\nspelling still emits exactly one fact and the two do not double-emit when both are\nwritten; a tree-sitter corpus case pins the block form's new `description` field. Each\ntest says which case fails when the change is backed out. cargo-test green via\nrustland/scripts/test.sh.\n\nREFERENCE: WI-1000 (measured it; the 059 R3 `describe` verdict that cites it),\nkernel-language.md §4.1 (description blocks), §5.4 (operation), `load_abstract_sort`\n/ `emit_desc_fact` as the working precedent."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-10T07:56:22Z"))
